1. What is the difference between getElementById, getElementsByClassName, and querySelector / querySelectorAll?

getElementById is used to select a specific element by its id and perform all necessary operations on it. Since an id is always unique, it returns only a single element.

getElementsByClassName is used to select all elements that have a specific class name and perform operations on them. Because the same class can be used on multiple elements, it returns multiple elements as a collection.

querySelectorAll is used to select multiple elements using a CSS selector. It returns a NodeList.

If document.querySelectorAll("body") is written, then the entire body element is selected.

